('chapter-340','chapter','Pronunciation Practice','Vowel Sounds and Review of Irregular Past Tense Verbs',35,'https://openoregon.pressbooks.pub/greentea/chapter/vowel-sounds-and-review-of-irregular-past-tense-verbs/','[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"American English Vowels"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Vowels are the sounds we make with our mouths open."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Maybe you know that we have 6 letters that are vowels: A E I O U and sometimes Y."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"When you say those letters, your mouth should be 100% open. Try it!"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"But, American English really has at least 12 vowel sounds. 😜"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"For example, you can hear 12 different vowels in these color words:"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"IPA"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"In dictionaries and English learning books, you will sometimes see special symbols to show the specific sounds. This can help you focus on the sound, and make sure other people can understand the way you pronounce the word."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"These special symbols are called IPA (International Phonetic Alphabet). For example, when we say the letter \"A\" in English, we write the IPA symbol / eɪ /."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"For example, the IPA for the word \"ate\" in most American accents is / eɪt /."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Focusing on the sounds can help you pronounce things easily, quickly, and clearly. Learning about the IPA is a good way to check your pronunciation. Some people learn the whole English IPA, too, and it helps them understand and organize English sounds."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Also, every English speaker has an accent. The same word may have many different pronunciations as you meet different people from different places. We can use IPA to show or write the different pronunciations."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Here is the list of color words again, with the IPA of the vowel sounds. Can you repeat just the vowel sound?"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Review: Vowels and Irregular Past Tense Verbs"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Verbs are action words, like:"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Many of the verbs we use the most in English are irregular past tense verbs. That means you have to memorize the past tense. You can''t say: doed, eated, or taked 😖. Instead, we say:"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This list has many of the irregular past tense verbs we use the most, organized by the vowel sounds. Can you read and repeat the verbs? Can you also say the present tense form of the words?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"You can listen and repeat, too."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"https://openoregon.pressbooks.pub/app/uploads/sites/92/2022/01/vowel-chart-green-tea-online.mp3"}]'::jsonb,339),
('chapter-242','chapter','Pronunciation Practice','Consonants: Voiced and Voiceless',36,'https://openoregon.pressbooks.pub/greentea/chapter/consonants-voiced-and-voiceless/','[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"A. Can you feel it?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Some English sounds are voiced . It means your vocal cords in your neck move when you make the sound."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Say: Aaaaaaaaaaaaaah. (Like at the doctor)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Some English sounds are voiceless . It means your vocal cords don’t move when you make the sound."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Say: Shhhhhhhhhhhhhhhh. (Like quieting your friend)."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"B. Read and repeat"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"https://openoregon.pressbooks.pub/app/uploads/sites/92/2020/07/voiceless-and-voiced.mp3"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"/t/ To tip feet retain"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"/k/ Coat come rack picture"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"/f/ Face fat half defend"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"/θ / Thin thick with nothing"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"/s/ Sue see price lesson"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"/ ʃ/ Shy show cash pressure"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"/ ʧ / chin choke natural catch /b/ Bear big rib rabbit"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"/d/ Do dip feed under"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"/g/ Goat gum rag sugar"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"/v/ Vase vat have clever"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"/ð/ That then neither father"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"/z/ Zoo Zip prize lazy"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"/ʒ / garage pleasure vision"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"C. Listen and circle (or read out loud with a tutor or teacher)"}]'::jsonb,147),
('chapter-249','chapter','Pronunciation Practice','Pronunciation: Adding S for plural and simple present third person',37,'https://openoregon.pressbooks.pub/greentea/chapter/pronunciation-adding-s-for-plural-and-simple-present-third-person/','[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Consonants: Final ‘s’ sounds"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"You can watch, listen, and repeat these sounds in Eric''s video:"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"English has three sounds for the letter “s.” You have to pay attention to the final sound before the s."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"A. Type one: Voiceless + /s/"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Verbs and nouns ending in voiceless consonants + /s/"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Practice: Circle or find the final sound before the s"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Example : takes The last sound before s is /k/"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Steps ____ looks____ laughs ____ fits_____"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"taps_____ fakes______ Eric’s ______"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"B. Type two: Voiced + /z/"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Verbs and nouns ending in voiced consonants + /z/"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Practice: Circle or find the final sound before the s"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Example : dogs The last sound before s is /g/"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Plans____ tags ____ halves ____ goes______ labels_____ climbs ___ stars ______ minds_____"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Plays _____ sees _____ sues ___ teacher’s _____"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"C. Type three: /ɪz/"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Verbs and nouns ending in /s/, /z/, sh/ʃ/, zh/ʒ/, ch/ʧ/, j/ʤ/, x/ks/ + /ɪz/ Practice: Circle or find the final sound before the s"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Example : misses The last sound before s is /s/"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"kisses_______ fizzes _____ wishes ______ garages _____"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"churches ____ judges ____ fixes ______ Chris’s _______"}]'::jsonb,188),
('chapter-251','chapter','Pronunciation Practice','Final S sounds practice',38,'https://openoregon.pressbooks.pub/greentea/chapter/251/','[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"D. Final S sounds practice"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Write the final sound and say the word"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"E.Read the sentences out loud."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Focus on the “s” pronunciation and voiced/voiceless sounds."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Practice, and record"}]'::jsonb,29),
('chapter-255','chapter','Pronunciation Practice','-ED pronunciation for Past Tense and Adjectives',39,'https://openoregon.pressbooks.pub/greentea/chapter/ed-pronunciation-for-past-tense-and-adjectives/','[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Type one: Voiceless + /t/"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"verbs and adjectives ending in voiceless consonants = /t/"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Stepped ____ looked______ laughed ____ wished ____"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Switched ___ clapped ____ kissed ______ faked _____"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Type two: Voiced + /d/"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"v erbs and adjectives ending in voiced consonants = /d/"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Planned ____ tagged ____ halved ____ rubbed ______ labeled_____ lazed _____ climbed ___ starred ______"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Played _____ peed _____ sued ___ pawed _____"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Type three: /d/ and /t/ + / ɪd/"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Verbs and adjectives ending in /t/ or /d + / ɪ d/"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Toasted _____ participated _____ faded ____ minded _____"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Practice out loud with a teacher or tutor:"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Focus on the final sounds: /t/, /d/ or / ɪ d/"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Find the -ed sound in these examples"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Focus on the ed words first. Then, read the sentence. Each sentence is an English proverb. Can you find the meaning with a tutor or teacher''s help?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Listen to Eric read, and repeat."}]'::jsonb,154),
('chapter-280','chapter','Pronunciation Practice','T Pronunciation',40,'https://openoregon.pressbooks.pub/greentea/chapter/t-pronunciation/','[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Type One: \"normal\" T"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA) symbol: [t]"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Notice the air that follows the t!"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Type 2: Flap T / Quick T"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This is a \"fast d\" sound. There is no extra air with the T! The IPA is: [d] or [ ɾ]"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This usually happens when we have a \"t sandwich\" — a vowel before the T and a vowel after the T."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Type 3: Glottal Stop T / ʔ/"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The glottal stop / ʔ/ is the stop of air in your voice. Example: uh-oh."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This kind of t sound often happens when there is a vowel + t + n, and the syllable after the t is not stressed."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Other spellings and sounds"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"T can change into some other sounds, too:"}]'::jsonb,126),
('chapter-338','chapter','Pronunciation Practice','Word Stress: Compound Words and Adj. + Noun Combinations',41,'https://openoregon.pressbooks.pub/greentea/chapter/word-stress-compound-words-and-adj-noun-combinations/','[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Eric Dodson"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"What is word stress?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Example: Portland Oregon"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"PORTland ORegon"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Oo Ooo <- The capital O means stressed syllables. The small o means unstressed syllables."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"not: portLAND oREgon"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Stress is:"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Practice by listening, repeating, and checking with a tutor or teacher:"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Compound Nouns"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The stress usually is on the first word or part:"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Normal Adjectives + Nouns"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The stress will usually fall on the noun. The adjective may have some stress, but it will have less than the noun — usually!"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"If you change the stress patterns, you may be changing the meaning or the feeling of what you''re saying."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Can you practice these common phrases with stress on the nouns?"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Extra: Stress on numbers!"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Numbers also almost always receive stress:"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Listen and Repeat"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"One good resource for teachers or tutors: British Council Teaching English - Word Stress"}]'::jsonb,139),
('chapter-327','chapter','Pronunciation Practice','Stress and Reductions and Example 1',42,'https://openoregon.pressbooks.pub/greentea/chapter/stress-and-reductions-in-sentences/','[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Stress + Reduced Syllables"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"We have talked about stressed syllables before. We can imagine that they are 100% whole and complete. Remember that stress means: louder, longer, higher, clearer, and bigger 😁"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Reduction is the opposite of stress in English."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Reduction, or reduced syllables are:"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"You can imagine that they are chopped up. Reduced stress makes it easier to put things together in spoken English, once you learn how."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This can help you with understanding other speakers, too. For example:"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Example 1: And"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"In normal American English, the word \"and\" usually reduces to \"an\" with a schwa sound: /ən/"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Here are some common phrases and idioms. Can you practice reducing \"and\"?"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"A. Reducing and"}]'::jsonb,114),
('chapter-349','chapter','Pronunciation Practice','Content words, function words, and stress',43,'https://openoregon.pressbooks.pub/greentea/chapter/content-words-function-words-and-stress/','[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Content Words"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Nouns, verb, adjectives and adverbs are usually stressed in sentences. Prepositions, articles and conjunctions are usually not stressed."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"You can usually understand the main ideas if you focus on the content words, too!"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"B. Which words are stressed in these sentences?"}]'::jsonb,43),
('chapter-351','chapter','Pronunciation Practice','Example Reduction: Her, hers, he, him, his',44,'https://openoregon.pressbooks.pub/greentea/chapter/example-reduction-her-hers-he-him-his/','[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Example Reductions: Her, Hers, He, Him, His"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"These pronouns usually loose the \"H\" in normal US English sentences. But, they keep the stress and the full \"H\" if they are the first word."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"C. Find these pronouns in this short conversation. Can you reduce them and combine them in a fluent sentence?"}]'::jsonb,52),
